  <h2>What a lien waiver is, and the four types</h2>
  <p>
    A mechanics lien is the strongest collection tool a contractor has: unpaid, you can attach a
    claim to the property itself. That leverage is exactly why owners and general contractors
    collect a lien waiver with every payment — a signed statement that, for the amount paid, you
    give up the right to lien (and usually your stop-notice and payment-bond rights). The four
    standard forms fall on a two-by-two grid:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Progress payment</th><th>Final payment</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Conditional</strong></td><td
          >Form 1 — covers one billing period; effective when that payment clears. Sign with the pay
          application.</td
        ><td
          >Form 3 — covers the whole contract including retention; effective when the final payment
          clears. Sign with the final application.</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Unconditional</strong></td><td
          >Form 2 — covers one billing period; effective on signing. Sign only after the payment has
          cleared.</td
        ><td
          >Form 4 — covers everything; effective on signing. Sign only after every dollar, retention
          included, has cleared.</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The <a href="/blog/construction-lien-waivers-explained/">lien waivers guide</a> explains the reasoning
    behind each; this page covers the forms themselves. Need only one kind? The
    <a href="/templates/conditional-lien-waiver/">conditional lien waiver</a> and
    <a href="/templates/unconditional-lien-waiver/">unconditional lien waiver</a> pages have each pair
    (progress and final) as its own download.
  </p>

  <h2>Which states require a statutory lien waiver form</h2>
  <p>
    Twelve states prescribe the wording of lien waivers by statute. In those states a waiver on any
    other form — including this one — may be ineffective or open to challenge, and a GC who sends
    you a non-statutory form there is usually making a mistake you should point out. Use the form in
    the statute or from the state agency, and verify it is the current version before signing.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>State</th><th>Statutory lien waiver forms</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        statutoryStates.map(([state, cite]) => (
          <tr>
            <>
              <td>{state}</td>
              <td>{cite}</td>
            </>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    In every other state the waiver form is set by the contract or by the GC — which is where these
    four general forms fit. Whatever form you use, match the waiver type to the payment: conditional
    until the check clears, unconditional only after. On a Florida private job, the waivers an owner
    collects before each payment come from the lienors who served a
    <a href="/templates/notice-to-owner/">notice to owner</a> — and they follow the § 713.20 statutory
    wording.
  </p>

  <h2>The forms, field by field</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Claimant, customer, owner, project</strong> — you; whoever contracted with you; the property
      owner; the property. The customer and the owner are different parties on every subcontract, and
      the waiver must name both.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Through date and payment amount</strong> — the period end and the current payment due on
      the pay application this waiver accompanies. The waiver language releases rights "through the through
      date" and "in the payment amount stated" and nothing more.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Maker of check and reference</strong> — who pays and which application or check it matches,
      so the waiver can be tied to a specific payment later.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Exceptions</strong> — the rights you keep. On the progress forms: retention withheld, pending
      change orders and extras, disputed claims, and anything furnished after the through date. On the
      final forms: disputed claims for additional work. Describe them in the box.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notice to claimant</strong> — printed in a box on the two unconditional forms: the document
      is enforceable when signed even if you have not been paid. It is there so nobody signs the wrong
      form by accident.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Signature and notary</strong> — printed name, title, company and date; notary acknowledgment
      optional unless the contract or the state requires it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example follows the same job as the
    <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/">pay application template</a>. Application no. 3 bills
    August: $286,805.00 completed and stored to date, $28,680.50 of retention withheld, and a
    current payment due of $122,557.50. The conditional progress waiver (form 1) therefore names the
    general contractor as the customer, the developer as the owner, a through date of August 31,
    2026, and a payment amount of $122,557.50 — with the $28,680.50 retention and a $5,784.50 change
    order that was submitted but not yet approved carved out in the exceptions. It is dated with the
    application. The unconditional progress waiver (form 2) carries the same figures and is dated a
    month later, when the payment cleared.
  </p>
  <p>
    The final forms are filled for closeout: a final application through November 30 releasing the
    $49,735.45 of retention on the $497,354.50 contract, all three change orders paid, no disputed
    claims. The conditional final goes with the final application and the
    <a href="/templates/certificate-of-completion/">certificate of substantial completion</a>; the
    unconditional final is signed after the retention check clears.
  </p>

  <h2>The sequence that protects you</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Submit the pay application with a conditional progress waiver for the current payment due.
    </li>
    <li>When the payment clears, sign the unconditional progress waiver if the GC requires one.</li>
    <li>
      Collect the same two waivers from your own sub-subcontractors and suppliers — most
      subcontracts make your payment conditional on them.
    </li>
    <li>
      At closeout, send the conditional final waiver with the final application and the closeout
      package.
    </li>
    <li>Sign the unconditional final only after the retention has cleared your account.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Your <a href="/templates/subcontractor-agreement/">subcontract</a> usually says which forms the GC
    expects and when; read that clause before the first application, and negotiate conditional waivers
    if the GC's template defaults to unconditional.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Signing an unconditional waiver before the check clears — the single costliest mistake.</li>
    <li>A progress waiver amount equal to the gross billed instead of the net paid.</li>
    <li>
      Leaving the exceptions blank, so retention and pending change orders are released with the
      period.
    </li>
    <li>Using a general form in one of the twelve statutory-form states.</li>
    <li>Naming the GC as the owner, or leaving the property address off.</li>
    <li>Not keeping the signed copy with the pay application it belongs to.</li>
  </ul>
